/* Track: Shocks, windfalls and ruin. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * The accumulation you never make is the one that gets erased, and the events
 * that do the erasing are a short list: income stopping, health failing, a
 * household splitting, and money arriving all at once. The evidence about what
 * actually happens comes first, then the instructions, because every
 * instruction in this track is a response written in advance and the response
 * is the part you cannot design while the event is happening. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "shocks-windfall-postpones-ruin",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "A large win postpones bankruptcy by about two years and does not make it any less likely",
  source: "Hankins, Hoekstra and Skiba, The Ticket to Easy Street? The Financial Consequences of Winning the Lottery (Review of Economics and Statistics, 2011)",
  gateIntro: "Someone in your family has just been paid out. It is a redundancy settlement worth about eighteen months of their salary, it landed on a Thursday, and by Saturday they have sent you a photograph of a caravan. They are not reckless and they are not stupid, and their credit card has carried a balance for three years. At the barbecue they ask you what they should do with it, and everybody at the table has a view.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction you would give them, phrased so that it names one payment the money could remove rather than one thing it could buy.",
  evidence: "Florida lottery winners linked to personal bankruptcy filings, comparing people who won a large prize against people who won a small one in the same lottery. That comparison is the design's whole value, because everybody in it bought a ticket, so the two groups differ in the size of the prize rather than in whatever makes a person play. Large winners, at prizes in the tens of thousands to the low hundreds of thousands of dollars, were less likely to file in the first couple of years after the win and more likely to file over the following few, and the cumulative filing rate across the whole window was indistinguishable between the groups. Large winners who did file arrived at the filing looking much like small winners who filed, which the authors read as the money having been spent rather than used to retire what was owed. One state, one lottery, and one very coarse outcome: bankruptcy is a legal event with its own thresholds rather than a measure of financial health. The prizes are also large against annual income and small against lifetime wealth, so nothing here speaks to what a genuinely life-changing sum does.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "A windfall arrives on the balance sheet and what failed was the income statement, so the money buys a known number of months and then the clock starts again.",
  why: "A stock and a flow are different objects and the failure being averted is a flow failure. Insolvency is what happens when obligations arriving each month exceed money arriving each month for long enough. A lump sum raises the level of the pond without changing the rate at which water leaves it, so what it buys is the lump divided by the monthly gap, in months, and at the end of those months the gap is the same gap it always was.\n\nIn one respect it is worse than neutral. Committed spending ratchets, and the things people buy when a large sum arrives tend to arrive with recurring costs attached: registration, insurance, maintenance, a bigger place, help for a relative that becomes a monthly help. So a non-recurring receipt gets converted into a permanently higher outflow, which is precisely the signature you would expect to produce a couple of quiet years followed by more filings rather than fewer.\n\nWhich makes every windfall question a flow question first, and there are only three answers that change the flow. Retire a debt whose repayment was part of the outflow. Buy something that produces income. Replace something whose failure would have created an outflow you cannot absorb. Everything else is time, and time is worth having, but it should be bought knowingly and counted in months rather than felt as relief.",
  failureMode: "A ninety thousand dollar payout covers a household's monthly shortfall for about fourteen months. In month fifteen the shortfall is the same shortfall, the card balance is back where it started, and the only durable changes are that the payout is gone and there is a caravan on the driveway costing several hundred dollars a year to register before anybody has towed it anywhere.",
  worked: "The instruction is an order of operations, and it gets written before any money arrives:\n\n\"Any unexpected sum over $5,000: before deciding anything, I write down my monthly money in and my monthly money out, and the first call on the money is whichever outflow it can remove permanently.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any target for the money and any mention of what it could buy. The question is not what to do with the lump. It is which recurring payment the lump can delete, because deleting a payment changes the flow and every other use of the money changes only the level.\n\nIf the honest answer is that no outflow can be removed, then the instruction says so out loud: the money is buying months, here is how many, and here is the date they run out.",
  experiment: "Take the last unexpected sum you received of any size, a tax refund or a bonus or a rebate or a settlement, and work out where it went. Most people can account for the first third and not the rest. Then write your current monthly inflow and outflow on the same page and identify the one recurring payment that a lump could permanently remove. That payment is your answer to the next windfall, decided in advance of it.",
  reflection: "Where did the last unexpected money you received actually go, and could you account for all of it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Large Florida winners were less likely to go bankrupt for a couple of years and more likely afterwards. What does that pattern tell you about what the money did?",
    a: "That it bought time rather than solvency. Insolvency follows from a gap between obligations arriving each month and money arriving each month, and a lump sum covers that gap for as long as it lasts without changing its size. Cover the shortfall for two years and filings fall for two years. Run out and they resume.\n\nThe cumulative filing rate over the whole window was indistinguishable between large and small winners, and the large winners who did file looked much like the small winners who filed by the time they got there. A lump also tends to raise the outflow, because the things people buy with a windfall carry recurring costs, which is how a one-off receipt becomes a permanently higher run rate."
  },
  deepDive: "I have an unexpected sum arriving and here is my monthly income and my committed spending. Tell me which recurring payment it could permanently remove, and what it costs me to spend it on anything else instead."
},
{
  id: "shocks-buffer-stock-target",
  track: "shocks", level: "standing",
  title: "Saving behaves like a buffer aimed at a target, which makes the refill after a shock the part worth automating",
  source: "Christopher Carroll, Buffer-Stock Saving and the Life Cycle/Permanent Income Hypothesis (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 1997)",
  gateIntro: "The hot water system went in June and took two thousand three hundred dollars out of the account you keep for exactly that. It worked. The account did the job it was there to do. It is now September, the balance is still short by the whole amount, and you have not thought about it once since the plumber drove away.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that refills the account without you having to notice that it is low, including the condition that starts it and the condition that stops it.",
  evidence: "A theoretical model matched against United States household data rather than an experiment, which is the first thing to know about it. Carroll shows that a consumer who is impatient and faces income risk they cannot insure does not accumulate toward a retirement target at all; they hold a target ratio of liquid wealth to permanent income, saving hard when they are below it and letting it drift when they are above. The model reproduces features of the data that the frictionless permanent-income model gets wrong, including the hump in consumption over the life cycle and the very low wealth of younger households. What it is not is an identified causal estimate. The target is a model object nobody observes directly, the impatience and income-risk parameters are fitted rather than measured, and the class of model has been extended in many directions since without a single decisive test of the target itself.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Your buffer has a resting level rather than a destination, so what a shock actually costs you is the correction, and the correction is what nobody schedules.",
  why: "The content of the model is that the amount of cash you hold is not a goal you are travelling toward but a level you return to. Saving is error correction around a ratio rather than monotone accumulation: above the line you let it slide, below it you push back. Most people recognise the description immediately once it is pointed at, which is the main reason to trust a calibration this heavily.\n\nThe part that matters here is what error correction needs in order to run. It needs the error to be noticed. Pushing back happens through spending less, which is a diffuse behaviour spread across a few hundred small decisions, and every one of those decisions depends on you registering that the balance is low. A shock is loud exactly once. It is loud on the day of the tradesman's invoice and silent for every month afterwards, and the silent months are when the refill was supposed to happen.\n\nSo the install is to take the correction out of the diffuse channel and put it in a scheduled one. The level already exists, whether or not you have written it down. Write it down, then arrange for the system rather than your attention to be the thing that notices a balance below it. In the months when nothing has gone wrong the rule costs nothing and does nothing, which is the property you want, because those are most of the months.",
  failureMode: "The buffer absorbs a two thousand three hundred dollar repair in June and is never refilled, so an account that held five months of fixed costs in May holds four in September and three by the following winter. Nothing went wrong. Each individual month simply had somewhere better for the money to go, and the level ratcheted down through three shocks across two years until the fourth one landed on a credit card.",
  worked: "Two clauses, and the second one is the entire entry:\n\n\"Hold the buffer at $18,000. In any month the balance is below $18,000, the automatic transfer that normally goes to investments goes to the buffer instead, and it switches back the month the buffer is whole.\"\n\nWhat is absent is any judgement about whether the withdrawal was justified. The rule does not ask why the balance fell and it does not distinguish a hot water system from a weekend away. That is deliberate, because a rule with a judgement in it requires somebody to make the judgement, and the reason the buffer is not being refilled is that nobody is looking at it.\n\nIf your bank will not switch a transfer conditionally, the manual approximation is one monthly reminder that opens the account and asks a single question, which is whether the number in front of you is below the number you wrote down.",
  experiment: "Find the real balance of whatever account is doing buffer duty and write it beside the level you think it should hold. If those two figures differ, work out which shock did it and what month that was. The gap between that month and today is how long your refill has been waiting on your attention, and it is usually longer than people guess. Then set the redirect rule, or the monthly check if your bank cannot do conditions.",
  reflection: "How far below its level was the buffer, and which shock had taken it there?",
  recall: {
    q: "Buffer-stock saving says households hold a target ratio of liquid wealth to income. Why does that make the refill rather than the balance the thing to automate?",
    a: "Because in the model the buffer is a resting level rather than a destination. Below it the precautionary motive pushes saving up and above it impatience pulls it down, so the behaviour is error correction around a ratio instead of steady accumulation. The balance immediately after a shock is not the problem. The correction that is supposed to follow is.\n\nAnd the correction runs through spending less, which is diffuse and depends entirely on you registering a low balance. A shock is loud on the day and silent afterwards, so the registering is exactly what fails. A rule that redirects an existing transfer until the buffer is whole replaces the noticing with a condition, and costs nothing in the months when nothing has happened."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my income, my fixed costs and the balance of the account I use as a buffer. Tell me what level it should hold and write me the redirect rule that refills it without me deciding anything."
},
{
  id: "shocks-derive-your-own-runway",
  track: "shocks", level: "standing",
  title: "Three to six months of expenses is a rule with no trial behind it, and your notice period gives you a better number",
  source: "The three-to-six-months emergency fund rule as it appears in consumer financial guidance, against the arithmetic of fixed costs, notice period and time to replacement income",
  gateIntro: "There is twelve thousand dollars in the account you think of as the emergency fund and you cannot remember how you arrived at twelve. Somebody said three to six months once, years ago, and it sounded about right. Your employment contract has a notice clause in it that you have not read since the day you signed it, and it is in a folder on a drive you would have to go looking for.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that sets the buffer to a number you derived rather than a number you inherited, with the two figures you multiplied together stated in it.",
  evidence: "No trial. The three-to-six-months figure is guidance rather than a finding: it appears across consumer education material, regulator pages and bank marketing with no study attached, and there is no randomised or quasi-experimental work establishing that a household holding three months of expenses fares better than one holding two or five. What is evidenced is adjacent and weaker than it sounds. Households with little liquid wealth show much larger consumption responses to income shocks and much higher rates of reported hardship, which supports holding a buffer and says nothing at all about how big it should be. The distribution of unemployment durations is also wide and strongly occupation-specific, so a single multiple of expenses cannot be right for everybody by construction. Notice what this entry is doing: asserting an absence of evidence, which is a claim about a literature rather than a result from one, and offering arithmetic in place of the missing trial rather than better evidence.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "The buffer you want is your fixed costs multiplied by the months between your last pay and your first replacement payment, and both of those figures are documents you already own.",
  why: "The rule multiplies the wrong quantity. Expenses for most households include a large discretionary component that stops in the same week income stops, because the second car sits still, the restaurants end and the holiday deposit does not get paid. A buffer sized on total spending is therefore sized for a month you will not be having. What has to be covered is the set of payments that arrive whether or not you are working: rent or mortgage, insurance, utilities, groceries, school fees, minimum repayments on anything owed.\n\nIt also multiplies by a duration nobody derived. The interval that matters is not a round number of months, it is the distance between your final pay and the first payment that replaces it, and that distance has components you can look up rather than guess. Your notice period. Any accrued leave paid out on exit. The waiting period on anything you hold that pays you when you cannot work. How long your last search actually took, which is the only number in the list with your own history behind it. Some of those shorten the interval and some lengthen it, and the answer is specific to you in a way that no multiple of expenses can be.\n\nDeriving it buys something larger than accuracy. A figure you computed from your own contract is one you can defend against yourself in a month when you would rather spend it, and a figure you inherited from guidance is not. The real failure of the three-to-six rule is not that it is wrong, because it is often close. It is that it belongs to nobody, and a number that belongs to nobody is soft the first time it is tested.",
  failureMode: "Twelve thousand dollars in an account you believe holds six months. Your fixed costs turn out to be four thousand one hundred a month, so it is under three, and your notice period is four weeks against a search that took you five months the last time you ran one. You have provided for roughly half the interval your own history says to expect, and you will find out which figure was right during the week you find out everything else.",
  worked: "One arithmetic sentence, then a transfer:\n\n\"My buffer is my fixed monthly costs times the months between my last pay and my first replacement payment. Fixed costs are rent, insurance, utilities, groceries, school fees and minimum repayments, which is $4,100. The interval is one month of notice plus the four months my last search took, so five. The buffer is $20,500, and the transfer that fills it is $500 the day after each pay until it gets there.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any allowance for discretionary spending, which the written cut order deals with instead, and any rounding up for comfort. The number is meant to be defensible rather than reassuring, and on this one those two properties pull in opposite directions.\n\nThe transfer is in there because a derived number with no instruction attached is a diary entry.",
  experiment: "Do the two lookups today. Both are documents you already have. Open your last three bank statements and add up only the payments that would still arrive if you stopped working on Friday. Then open your employment contract and read the notice clause, which takes about a minute and which most people have never done twice. Multiply the first figure by the second plus however long your last job search really took, and write the result beside what the account currently holds.",
  reflection: "What was your actual fixed monthly cost, and how far was it from what you had been assuming?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the buffer account hold the number I derived today, or more, in ninety days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "A derived buffer and the three-to-six-months rule often produce similar figures. Why is the derived one still better?",
    a: "Because the rule multiplies total expenses by a duration nobody derived, while the derived version multiplies the payments that arrive whether or not you are working by the actual interval between your last pay and your first replacement payment. Discretionary spending stops when income stops, so sizing a buffer on it provides for a month you will not be having, and the interval has components you can look up: notice period, leave paid out on exit, waiting periods on anything that pays you when you cannot work, and the length of your last search.\n\nThe second reason is ownership. There is no trial behind three to six months, and a figure inherited from guidance is one you will argue yourself out of in a month when you want the money for something else. A figure you computed from your own contract is one you can hold the line on."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my fixed monthly costs, my notice period, my leave balance and how long my last job search took. Derive my buffer number, then tell me what it is that this number does not provide for."
},
{
  id: "shocks-pre-decided-cut-order",
  track: "shocks", level: "standing",
  title: "The order in which spending gets cut has to be written before income stops, because during the event you will cut nothing",
  source: "Pre-commitment and if-then planning applied to household spending, and the operational practice behind redundancy and financial hardship checklists",
  gateIntro: "Your area was restructured on a Tuesday and three people who sit near you were gone by Friday. Nothing has happened to you. You spend the weekend running numbers in your head at three in the morning, and by Monday you have concluded that if it comes to it you will cut back, which is the same phrase you used the last time a round of this went through.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the first three lines of the list, in the order you would actually run them, with a dollar amount or a phone call beside each one.",
  evidence: "No trial. Nobody has run an experiment on a household spending cut order, so this entry cannot rest on one. The adjacent literature is on if-then planning, where specifying the situation and the response in advance has been found across a great many studies to raise the rate at which intentions get acted on, at effect sizes that are moderate and that later work has argued are inflated by small samples and publication bias. Even taken at its most generous, none of it involves money, a job loss or a household budget. The other support is operational rather than empirical: financial counsellors, lender hardship teams and redundancy checklists all work from a prepared sequence rather than improvising, and the reason they give is that the person in front of them cannot prioritise under the pressure of the event. That is a professional consensus, which is worth something and is not a result.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Decide in writing, now, what stops and in what order on the day the income stops, because the sequencing is the one part you cannot do while it is happening.",
  why: "Consider what competes for attention during an income shock. There is the conversation at home, the paperwork, the applications, the people who have to be told, the sleep you are not getting. Spending decisions sit at the bottom of that queue because they never feel urgent on any particular day, and the observable result is that nothing gets cut for weeks while the account drains at the old rate, followed by a burst of cuts made under pressure in the third month.\n\nThe order matters more than the total, because the items differ in what they cost to reverse. Some are free and undoable: a paused investment transfer, eleven subscriptions, the extra repayment. Some are cheap and unpleasant: the restaurants, the holiday, the grocery figure. Some are expensive or permanent: selling an asset into a bad week, breaking a lease, drawing on retirement savings, cancelling cover you may not be able to buy again if your health has changed. And exactly one item is worth more than all the others together, which is speaking to a lender before a payment is missed rather than after, because the options on offer before a default and after one are different sets. Somebody improvising tends to run that sequence close to backwards.\n\nThe list also does something on the afternoon you write it, before any shock exists. It tells you the true size of the flexible part of your spending, and the number is usually smaller than people expect and takes longer to realise than they expect. Knowing it changes what you think your buffer has to cover, which is the other half of the same arithmetic.",
  failureMode: "Six weeks between the redundancy conversation and the first cut of any kind. At four thousand one hundred a month of fixed cost plus a spending pattern you had already decided to abandon, that is on the order of six thousand dollars of buffer spent maintaining a life you had privately given up on. Then the cuts arrive in the order of whatever is easiest to cancel that afternoon, and the mortgage conversation happens after the first missed payment rather than before it, which is a different conversation with a different set of options in it.",
  worked: "A list with a trigger sentence on the front, kept somewhere you will actually find it:\n\n\"On the day I am told, in this order: pause the transfer to investments but not the transfer to the buffer; cancel the eleven subscriptions listed on page two; stop the extra mortgage repayment; ring the lender and say that I have lost my income and want to know my hardship options, before any payment is missed; move the grocery budget to the lower figure; and leave every insurance policy exactly where it is.\"\n\nTwo things are in there on purpose. The lender call is placed before any payment is missed, because it is the only item whose value collapses if it happens later. And the insurance is protected explicitly, because it is the line most likely to be cut by somebody improvising and the one that cannot be bought back if your health has changed in the meantime.\n\nWhat is absent is judgement. The list does not ask whether things are serious enough yet, because that question is how six weeks go past.",
  experiment: "Write the list today, on one page, and store it with your will or your passport rather than in an app note you will not find. It needs three things: the trigger sentence that starts it, the items in order with real dollar amounts beside them, and your lender's phone number written out. Then total the flexible column, because that figure is your genuine month-to-month flexibility and most people have never once seen it.",
  reflection: "What did the flexible column total, and was it larger or smaller than you had assumed?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I be able to put my hand on the written cut order inside two minutes, six months from now?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why does the order of the cuts matter more than the total they save?",
    a: "Because the items differ in reversibility and in whether their value decays. Pausing an investment transfer or cancelling subscriptions is free and undoable. Selling an asset in a bad week, breaking a lease, drawing on retirement savings or cancelling cover you may not be able to re-underwrite are expensive or permanent. And one line is worth more than the rest combined, which is speaking to a lender before a payment is missed, because the options available before a default are not the options available after one.\n\nSomebody improvising in the third month tends to run that sequence backwards, cutting whatever is easiest to cancel that afternoon and making the lender call after the damage. There is no trial behind the ordering. The professions that do this work all use a prepared sequence, and the reason they give is that nobody can prioritise during the event itself."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my spending, my mortgage and my insurance. Write my cut order for the day I lose my income, in the sequence you would actually run it, and tell me which item I would get wrong if I were improvising."
},
{
  id: "shocks-transfers-get-spent",
  track: "shocks", level: "standing",
  title: "A one-off payment gets spent fast, so its destination has to be decided before it arrives",
  source: "Parker, Souleles, Johnson and McClelland, Consumer Spending and the Economic Stimulus Payments of 2008 (American Economic Review, 2013), with Johnson, Parker and Souleles, Household Expenditure and the Income Tax Rebates of 2001 (American Economic Review, 2006)",
  gateIntro: "Your tax refund lands on a Tuesday in July. It is about four thousand dollars, you knew roughly what it would be, and you had said in June that this year it was going somewhere sensible. It is now the second week of August. The account is back to its usual balance, the refund is not in the savings account, and you could name maybe three of the things it turned into.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the rule that decides where the next non-salary credit goes, with the split written as percentages and no approval step in it.",
  evidence: "Two natural experiments in the same design. The timing of the 2008 United States stimulus payments was determined by the last two digits of the recipient's social security number, which makes receipt-week effectively random, so households paid early can be compared against otherwise similar households paid weeks later. Spending was measured in the Consumer Expenditure Survey, which added a module asking about the payments. Households spent a substantial share of the payment quickly, on the order of a quarter of it on non-durable goods within the quarter it arrived and a considerably larger share once vehicles and other durables are counted, and the response was concentrated in households with little liquid wealth. The 2001 rebates, identified the same way, gave the same pattern. Limits worth carrying: survey-reported spending is measured with error, durables purchases are partly saving in another form rather than pure consumption, the payments were modest and announced as one-off, and this is the United States in and around a recession, where the response to extra cash may not be typical of a household in a normal year.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Decide where an unexpected payment goes before it arrives: randomly timed one-off transfers get largely spent inside the quarter they land.",
  idea: "Money that arrives outside your salary has no default attached to it, and the evidence says the absence of a default is resolved by spending.",
  why: "Start with why the response is so fast, because it is not a failure of character. The spending was concentrated in households with little liquid wealth, which means the payment was not competing against a plan. It was relieving a constraint that had been binding for months, against a queue of deferred purchases that the money simply cleared. That is the buffer-stock account running forwards rather than any kind of recklessness, and it would happen to a careful person on the same terms.\n\nWhat makes it your problem is narrower. Your salary already has a default: it arrives on a known date and something automatic happens to part of it. A refund, a bonus, a rebate, a reimbursement, a dividend, an insurance settlement, a gift, a distribution on an asset sale, none of those usually has one. They land in the everyday account, and whatever is in front of you that fortnight decides. The leak is not proportional to your discipline, it is proportional to how much of your annual income arrives irregularly.\n\nSo the fix has the same shape as the salary fix and it is easier, because the money has not arrived yet and is therefore cheap to commit. A percentage split, written once, applying to any credit over a threshold, weighted toward the buffer rather than toward investments. Weighted that way because unexpected money is by definition evidence that your model of your own cash flow has error in it, and the account that absorbs error is the buffer.",
  failureMode: "A four thousand dollar refund arrives every July and is gone by mid August, spread across a dozen purchases you would not defend individually and cannot now list. Six years of that is twenty-four thousand dollars that passed through the account without ever once being a decision, while the same six years were spent trying to find forty dollars a fortnight in the grocery budget.",
  worked: "One rule, and it covers every case so you never have to decide which case you are in:\n\n\"Any credit into my account that is not salary and is over $1,000 gets split on the day it lands: eighty percent to the buffer account, twenty percent spent on whatever I want, no approval needed for either half.\"\n\nThe twenty percent is not a concession, it is the load-bearing part. A rule with no spending allowance in it gets broken on the first bonus and then abandoned wholesale, because the breach is treated as evidence that the rule was unrealistic. Eighty percent that survives ten years beats a hundred percent that survived one refund.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a list of qualifying events. Naming the events invites the argument about whether this particular arrival counts, and that argument is always won by the money.",
  experiment: "List every non-salary credit that arrived in your accounts over the last twelve months: refunds, bonuses, rebates, reimbursements over a hundred dollars, dividends, gifts, insurance payments, sale proceeds. Your bank's search will do most of it. Add them up. That total is the money that has been passing through with no default attached, and it is usually larger than the amount people are trying to squeeze out of everyday spending. Then write the split rule and set the destination account so the transfer is two taps.",
  reflection: "What did the non-salary credits of the last year add up to, and how much of it can you still account for?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I be able to say exactly where the next unexpected payment over one thousand dollars went, ninety days after it lands?" },
  recall: {
    q: "The 2008 payments were timed by the last two digits of a social security number. Why does that detail carry the whole result?",
    a: "Because it makes the week of receipt effectively random, so households paid early can be compared with otherwise similar households paid weeks later. Without it you are comparing spending before and after a payment during a recession, and that comparison is contaminated by everything else happening in those quarters.\n\nWhat the comparison showed was a fast and substantial spending response, concentrated in households with little liquid wealth, which is a binding constraint being relieved rather than a failure of will. The consequence for you is that the destination of a one-off payment has to be fixed before it arrives, because in the week it lands there is no default operating and whatever is in front of you decides."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every non-salary credit that hit my accounts last year. Write me one routing rule that covers all of them, and pick the split I will not abandon after the first bonus."
},
{
  id: "shocks-unsourced-ruin-statistic",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "The claim that most windfall recipients end up with nothing has no study behind it, and the real finding is more useful",
  source: "The widely repeated claim that a large majority of lottery winners lose everything, traced against the windfall literature that does exist: Hankins, Hoekstra and Skiba on bankruptcy timing, and the Swedish register work by Cesarini, Lindqvist, Notowidigdo and Ostling",
  gateIntro: "You are in a conversation about somebody at work who has come into money, and a person at the table says the thing everybody says, which is that seventy percent of these people end up with nothing. Everyone nods. You nod. And you notice, nodding, that you have said the same figure out loud at least twice yourself and could not name where you first heard it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the rule you would use before repeating a financial number, including what you do with the number when the rule fails.",
  evidence: "The figure circulates without a primary source. Follow any instance of it and the citation leads to an article citing another article citing an organisation rather than a paper, and the trail stops there; it is usually attributed to a United States financial education body rather than to any study with a sample and a method. What the actual literature supports is narrower and more interesting. Hankins, Hoekstra and Skiba find that large Florida prizes shifted the timing of bankruptcy without changing its eventual rate, which is an effect on when rather than a majority losing everything. The Swedish register work finds windfall wealth persisting for many years with labour supply barely moving, which is close to the opposite of dissipation. So a real phenomenon has been dressed in a number nobody can source. Note the shape of this entry's own claim: it asserts the absence of a citation, which cannot be proved, only checked, and you should check it rather than take it from here.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "An unsourced number can be worse than no number, because it makes the hazard vivid and points it at the wrong thing.",
  why: "Consider what believing the folk figure does to your attention. It describes spectacular self-destruction, which is a category almost nobody assigns themselves to, so the risk gets filed under other people's recklessness and dismissed in the same motion. It also implies that the danger is a single dramatic event, which means you watch for the boat and the casino and not for anything that would actually happen to you.\n\nNow put the real findings in its place, and notice they point in two directions at once. Large prizes postponed insolvency without preventing it, which says the risk is a flow that was never fixed. And Swedish winners kept most of their wealth for a decade or more while barely changing their working lives, which says dissipation is not the modal outcome at all. Both of those are undramatic and both are actionable. Recurring costs created by a one-off receipt. Money relocated between accounts and mistaken for money saved. Tax and family obligations that arrive a year after the decision that created them.\n\nThe general habit is worth more than this particular correction, because the folk statistic has a recognisable shape: a large round percentage, a dramatic outcome, no method. When a number would change what you do, find the study or stop repeating the number, and keep the mechanism, which usually survives. Windfalls really do get converted into run rate. That part is evidenced. It just does not need seventy percent to be true.",
  failureMode: "A year of repeating a statistic to your children and your colleagues, and then a real windfall arrives and your caution is aimed at the wrong hazard entirely. You do not lose it at a casino. You upgrade the car, take on two family commitments that recur monthly, and buy a place sized on the deposit rather than on the income, and by the second year the outflow is eight hundred dollars a month higher against income that never moved.",
  worked: "This one produces a filter rather than a transfer:\n\n\"Before I repeat a financial number, I look for the study. If the trail ends at an article or an organisation rather than at a sample and a method, I stop using the number and keep the mechanism.\"\n\nThe second half of that sentence is the part people skip, and dropping it turns a useful habit into a way of dismissing things. The mechanism almost always survives the collapse of the number. Abandoning seventy percent does not mean abandoning caution about a large sum, it means aiming the caution at a rising outflow instead of at a catastrophe.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any requirement to find the correct figure. You are not going to. Ten minutes and a decision about whether you keep saying it is the whole procedure.",
  experiment: "Pick the financial statistic you repeat most often, in either direction, frightening or reassuring. Spend ten minutes trying to reach a sample size and a method. Ten minutes is the entire exercise, not a research project. If you cannot get there, write the number on a short list of things you no longer say, and write beside it the mechanism you still believe. The list is usually shorter than people fear and the frightening numbers are heavily over-represented on it.",
  reflection: "Which number did you try to trace, and did it survive ten minutes?",
  recall: {
    q: "The unsourced claim and the real windfall literature both counsel caution about a large sum. Why does it matter which one you are carrying?",
    a: "Because they point at different hazards and therefore at different responses. The folk figure describes spectacular dissipation, which files the risk under other people's recklessness and exempts you the moment you hear it, and it implies the danger is one dramatic event. The evidence describes something quiet: a lump postponing an insolvency whose cause it never touched, and windfall wealth that largely persists while working lives barely change.\n\nSo the caution the evidence supports is aimed at the flow. Recurring costs created by a one-off receipt, relocation of existing money mistaken for saving, and obligations that arrive a year after the decision. None of that looks like losing everything, and all of it is what actually happens."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are three financial statistics I repeat. For each one tell me whether there is a study behind it, and what the mechanism is that survives even if the number does not."
},
{
  id: "shocks-windfall-and-working",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "Winning enough to stop working mostly does not stop people working, and what it does change it changes slowly",
  source: "Cesarini, Lindqvist, Notowidigdo and Ostling, The Effect of Wealth on Individual and Spousal Labor Supply: Evidence from Swedish Lotteries (American Economic Review, 2017)",
  gateIntro: "A friend asks you the pub question: what would you do if three years of your after-tax pay landed tomorrow. You give the answer everybody gives, which involves handing in a notice period, and then somebody at the table who actually inherited a sum like that four years ago says nothing at all and keeps going to work on Mondays.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that compares what you spend protecting your earning capacity against what you spend optimising your investments, as two figures you could put side by side this afternoon.",
  evidence: "Swedish lottery players linked to administrative tax and earnings registers, with prizes random conditional on the number of tickets a person held, which is what makes winners and non-winners comparable rather than merely similar. Winning reduced subsequent labour earnings, and the reduction was modest, showed up mainly as somewhat fewer hours and somewhat earlier retirement rather than as quitting, persisted for around a decade, and absorbed only a small fraction of the prize in forgone earnings. Spousal earnings fell slightly as well. What limits it: Sweden has a compressed wage distribution and a strong safety net, so the size of an income effect on work there need not transfer; lottery players are not a random sample of the population; the prizes are large against annual earnings but mostly not life-altering; and earnings registers see paid work rather than hours, effort or anything unpaid.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "People predict money would buy them out of work and the record says they buy a little of it, slowly, which makes your earning capacity a far more durable asset than you probably treat it as.",
  why: "Take the work decision apart and the result stops being surprising. A wage buys the mortgage and it also delivers structure, colleagues, identity, competence and a reason to be somewhere on a Tuesday. A lump sum removes the financial necessity and leaves every one of the others standing, so what you would expect is a small adjustment at the margin, taken slowly, in the direction of fewer hours and an earlier finish. That is what shows up in the registers.\n\nThe gap between that and the pub answer is the interesting part, because it is a measurable error people make about themselves, in a domain where they have strong intuitions and no feedback. It belongs beside the other error this site keeps returning to, which is that stated intentions about money predict very little about behaviour. Here the direction of the error is at least flattering: people are more attached to work than they think.\n\nFor a track about shocks, the consequence is the one worth keeping. Wealth barely displaced earnings for people who had every financial reason to stop, which is evidence about how large the earnings stream is relative to the stock most people can accumulate. And that stream is exactly what every shock in this track attacks: the illness, the redundancy, the household splitting, the injury. Protecting the earning capacity therefore outranks optimising the portfolio for most people at most points, and it is the item that receives the least attention because it does not have a return you can quote.",
  failureMode: "A plan whose whole shape rests on stopping work at a number you have never tested, while the asset generating nearly all of your net worth is an earning capacity you have never insured, never audited, and maintain by accident. The Swedish winners had the money and kept working anyway. You have the working, and no cover on it, and a spreadsheet about the other thing.",
  worked: "A comparison rather than a transfer, and it fits on one line:\n\n\"Write down the annual cost of everything I do to protect or extend my earning capacity, and the annual cost of everything I do to optimise my investments, and put the two figures side by side.\"\n\nThe first column is premiums, training, professional currency, health maintenance, the network. The second is platform fees, adviser fees, the hours, the reading. Most people find the second is a multiple of the first, and the first is often zero, which is a strange allocation for anybody whose wealth is mostly a wage.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a recommended ratio, because nobody knows what it should be. Making the ratio visible is the whole instruction, on the grounds that a ratio nobody has looked at is not a decision anybody made.",
  experiment: "Write down what you honestly believe you would do about work if a sum equal to three years of your after-tax income arrived tomorrow. Then write what the register data says people in roughly that position did, which is work a bit less for a long time. If your accumulation plan has a stopping point in it, the distance between those two lines is the error in the plan's premise. Then do the two-column comparison and see which column you have been funding.",
  reflection: "What did you predict you would do about work, and which of the two columns has been getting your money?",
  recall: {
    q: "Swedish lottery winners did reduce their labour supply, so why is the result usually described as surprising?",
    a: "Because the reduction was small, gradual and persistent rather than a stopping point. Earnings fell modestly, mostly through somewhat fewer hours and somewhat earlier retirement rather than through quitting, spouses' earnings fell slightly, and the forgone earnings absorbed only a small share of the prize. That is a long way from what people say they would do with the same money, and work delivers structure, identity and competence that a lump sum does not replace.\n\nThe design is what makes it credible: prizes were random conditional on tickets held, and earnings came from administrative registers rather than self-report. The limits are Sweden's compressed wages and strong safety net, and lottery players not being a random sample, so the magnitude of the income effect should not be exported wholesale."
  },
  deepDive: "My plan assumes I stop working at a certain number. Pressure-test that premise against what people with sudden wealth actually do, and tell me what the plan should be protecting instead."
},
{
  id: "shocks-satisfaction-not-mood",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "Windfall wealth lifts how satisfied people are with their lives for years and barely touches how they feel day to day",
  source: "Lindqvist, Ostling and Cesarini, Long-Run Effects of Lottery Wealth on Psychological Well-Being (Review of Economic Studies)",
  gateIntro: "You are being offered a role that pays substantially more. It also involves a commute that adds ninety minutes to your day, a team in another time zone, and the end of the two mornings a week you currently swim. Your partner asks whether it will make you happier. You say yes, and then you sit with the answer for a moment, because you are not sure which sense of the word you meant.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the rule that decides which kinds of money decisions get made on the money, and which kinds do not, in terms specific enough to apply to this offer.",
  evidence: "A survey of Swedish lottery players linked to prize records and administered years after the wins, often a decade or more, measuring life satisfaction on one hand and measures of momentary mood and mental health on the other. Large-prize winners reported clearly and durably higher life satisfaction; the estimated effects on happiness measured as recent affect, and on mental-health indicators, were small and not reliably distinguishable from zero. The design has real limits. These are self-reported scales whose units resist interpretation. Winners were surveyed long after the event, so who answered and what they recalled both matter. It is Sweden again, and the same lottery population as the labour-supply work. And a survey at one point in time cannot show the trajectory in between, only where people had arrived.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "A large sum durably changes your assessment of your life and does not change your afternoon, and knowing which of those you are buying prevents most expensive money decisions.",
  why: "The two measures are asking different questions and are made of different inputs. Life satisfaction is an evaluation delivered on request: you step back, take the whole thing in, and compare it against expectations and against other people. Money moves that, because it moves what people evaluate against, which is security, options and the set of futures still available. Momentary mood is a report on the last hour, and the last hour is made of sleep, pain, the commute, the argument, the deadline and who was in the room. A balance does not enter it.\n\nThe collapse of that distinction is expensive in both directions, and each direction has a slogan attached. Money does not buy happiness gets used to argue against building security, when the durable satisfaction effect is precisely what security appears to buy. Money buys happiness gets used to justify trading away sleep, health and the people you see, which are the actual inputs to daily mood, in pursuit of a figure that would have improved the evaluation and left the afternoons where they were.\n\nIn a track about shocks this locates the return on a boring instruction. A buffer does not make your Tuesday better and it is not supposed to. What it does is remove a class of possible futures, and removing them is the thing that shows up years later as a higher answer to how your life is going. That return is real, it is slow, and it is invisible on any statement, which is why the instruction has to be automatic rather than motivated.",
  failureMode: "Two years in a role that pays forty thousand more and costs you the swimming, the sleep and most of your weekends, taken on the theory that the money would make you happier. The evaluation does improve, which is the cruel part, because it makes the decision defensible in the abstract. The Tuesdays do not improve, and the inputs you traded away are exactly what Tuesdays are built from.",
  worked: "The instruction sorts decisions into two bins rather than pricing a trade between them:\n\n\"Any change that raises my income while cutting into my sleep, my health or the time I spend with people gets decided on those three and not on the money. Any change that raises my security without touching those three gets decided on the money, quickly, without a discussion.\"\n\nThe second sentence matters as much as the first. Most of the security decisions in this track are small, dull and unopposed, and they get deferred anyway because they feel like they deserve consideration. They do not. Consideration is for the first bin.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is an exchange rate between the two bins. Nobody has measured one, so the honest procedure is to keep the categories separate rather than to pretend the trade can be priced.",
  experiment: "Write down the last two decisions you made partly on money grounds, and mark each one according to whether what it changed was your evaluation of your life or the content of your days. Most people find at least one where a security argument was used to justify a cost to daily life, and that is the decision worth reopening this week, rather than anything in the portfolio.",
  reflection: "Which of your money decisions was really about your Tuesdays, and did you argue for it as though it were about security?",
  recall: {
    q: "Lottery wealth raised life satisfaction for years and barely moved measures of momentary happiness. Why is that distinction worth keeping hold of?",
    a: "Because the two are different measurements with different inputs. Life satisfaction is an evaluation made on request, and money moves it because it moves what people evaluate against: security, options, the futures still on the table. Momentary mood reports on the last hour, and the last hour is made of sleep, pain, the commute and the people in it, none of which a balance reaches.\n\nCollapsing them costs you in both directions. It licenses arguing against building security, which is the thing the satisfaction result suggests money does deliver and keeps delivering. And it licenses trading away sleep, health and relationships for an income that improves the evaluation while degrading the very inputs daily mood is built from."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the two big money decisions in front of me. Sort them into ones that change my evaluation of my life and ones that change my days, and tell me which I should not be deciding on the money at all."
},
{
  id: "shocks-ruin-is-not-symmetric",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "A permanent loss and an equal percentage gain are not the same size, which is the entire argument against a stake you cannot rebuild",
  source: "The gambler's ruin problem and Kelly's criterion for bet sizing, from Kelly's 1956 work on information rate, both of which are arithmetic rather than empirical findings",
  gateIntro: "Your partner's brother needs eighty thousand dollars of a business loan guaranteed and the bank will not proceed without a second signature. He has run the place for nine years, he has never missed a payment, and he is standing in your kitchen on a Tuesday night. Both families will hear about this either way, and he needs an answer before Friday.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the rule you would have wanted in place before he asked, phrased as a ceiling on what a single decision may put at risk.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is arithmetic and it neither has nor needs a study behind it. Two results carry it. Gambler's ruin: a player betting repeatedly against a counterparty with deeper reserves goes broke with probability approaching one as the number of bets grows, even on a favourable expectation, unless the bet is scaled down as the stake shrinks. And Kelly's criterion, which gives the fraction of a bankroll that maximises the long-run growth rate and which shrinks toward nothing as the chance of losing the lot rises, because a strategy capable of taking everything in one round takes everything given enough rounds regardless of its expected value. What the arithmetic cannot supply is your own probability of ruin, which is the input that actually matters and the one nobody has. So treat this as a constraint on which decisions are admissible at all, rather than as a formula that sizes them.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Wealth compounds multiplicatively, so a loss and an equal gain do not cancel, and a total loss cannot be undone by any return at all.",
  why: "Start with the asymmetry, because it is the part people nod at and then forget. Down fifty percent and up fifty percent leaves you at seventy-five. Down eighty needs four hundred to recover. At a total loss the asymmetry stops being a matter of degree, because zero multiplied by any subsequent return is still zero, and every plan you have that depends on time in the market depends on the path never touching it. Expected return tells you almost nothing once the path can pass through nothing.\n\nThe stake at risk in a household is rarely the portfolio, which is what makes this hard to see. The events that produce ruin in real life are not called bets: a guarantee signed for somebody you love, a business structure that lets a creditor reach personal assets, a holding in the same employer that pays your salary, borrowing sized on two incomes that a single event removes, a liability nobody insured. Each has a low annual probability and a total-loss payoff, which is exactly the shape the arithmetic rules out.\n\nSo the useful output is a filter rather than an optimiser. No decision may be capable of removing the thing that would let you rebuild, where the thing is the buffer, the roof and the ability to earn. And the correlations matter more than the sizes, because if your salary, your equity holding, your house and your industry are all exposed to the same conditions, you are holding one position wearing four costumes and the individual probabilities you have been comforting yourself with do not multiply the way you think.",
  failureMode: "You guarantee the eighty thousand, because the chance of it being called really is small. Four years later it is called in the same quarter that the industry turns, which is not a coincidence but a correlation you never priced, and the eighty thousand comes out of the buffer and then out of the offset. The expected value of the guarantee was mildly positive. The realised version removed six years of accumulation and most of what would have let you rebuild it.",
  worked: "The instruction is a ceiling, and it is phrased in terms of what survives rather than what is at stake:\n\n\"No single decision may put at risk more than I could rebuild in two years of saving at my current rate, and anything capable of reaching the house, the buffer or my ability to work is out regardless of the numbers attached to it.\"\n\nThe second clause is the load-bearing one, because the first invites arithmetic and arithmetic is where people talk themselves into things. A ceiling denominated in rebuild time is also checkable without a calculator: you know your savings rate, so you know your ceiling, and it moves with your circumstances without needing to be revised.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any reference to the probability of the bad outcome. You do not have that number, nobody has offered you a calibrated one, and the events on the list are correlated with each other in ways that make the individual probabilities close to meaningless.",
  experiment: "List everything currently capable of taking more than two years of your saving in a single event: a guarantee, a directorship, a sole-trader structure, a concentrated holding, a property whose repayments assume two incomes, an uninsured liability. Then mark which of them would fire under the same conditions as the others. The count matters less than the correlation, and most people find they are holding one exposure that appears three times on the list.",
  reflection: "What is currently capable of removing more than two years of your saving in one event?",
  recall: {
    q: "Expected value says a mildly favourable bet is worth taking. Why does that reasoning fail for a bet that could take the whole stake?",
    a: "Because wealth compounds multiplicatively and losses do not cancel symmetrically. Down fifty percent then up fifty percent leaves you at seventy-five, down eighty needs four hundred to recover, and a total loss cannot be undone by any return at all, since zero multiplied by anything is zero. A strategy capable of taking everything in one round takes everything given enough rounds, whatever its expected value, which is the gambler's ruin result and the reason Kelly sizing shrinks toward nothing as the chance of total loss rises.\n\nThe practical version is that the constraint is on admissibility rather than on size. No decision may be capable of removing what would let you rebuild, and because household exposures are correlated - the salary, the holding, the house and the industry can all be one bet - the individual probabilities are a poor defence."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my balance sheet, my employment and any guarantees or liabilities I have signed. Tell me what is capable of taking more than two years of my saving in one event, and which of those would fire at the same time as each other."
},
{
  id: "shocks-insurance-triage",
  track: "shocks", level: "annual",
  title: "Insure what is rare and unaffordable, pay for what is common and affordable, and most households have it the other way round",
  source: "Insurance triage by frequency and severity, as practised in risk management rather than established by any trial",
  gateIntro: "You are at the counter buying a dishwasher and the person serving you offers four years of cover for a hundred and ninety dollars. You say yes, because it is under fifty a year against a machine you cannot do without, and it takes about eleven seconds. There are three other policies of roughly that shape sitting on your bank statement and you have never added them up.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the annual instruction that decides which premiums you keep, phrased as two questions you can answer without looking anything up.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is craft: a two-by-two that risk managers use to allocate cover, where high frequency with low severity is absorbed, low frequency with high severity is transferred, and the two off-diagonal cases are argued about case by case. The reasoning behind it is arithmetic rather than empirical. An insurer must charge more than the expected loss in order to cover its own costs and capital, so across many small claims the insured party reliably pays a loading for a loss they could have carried themselves. What they are buying correctly is the deletion of outcomes they could not carry at all. What has no evidence behind it is the placement of any particular risk in the grid, which depends entirely on your own balance sheet, and there is no study showing that households which triage this way finish wealthier than households which do not.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Insurance is a bad deal on average and that is not an argument against it, because you are buying the removal of the outcomes that end the plan rather than a positive expected value.",
  why: "Every policy is priced above its expected loss, and it has to be, or the insurer does not exist. So anybody buying insurance is knowingly accepting a negative expected value in return for cutting off a tail. That trade is correct exactly when the tail would remove your capacity to continue, and incorrect when the loss is one you could write a cheque for without changing anything else about the month.\n\nWhich turns the whole question into two questions with no judgement in them. How often would this happen. Could I pay for the worst version out of the buffer without altering anything else. Extended warranties, screen cover, appliance plans, an excess set as low as it goes, cover for routine veterinary care: all frequent, all survivable, all of them a loading paid for the convenience of somebody else handling a cost you can carry. Cover on your income, on total and permanent disability, on your life where somebody depends on it, on liability, on the house against catastrophe: rare, and the severity is measured in years of your life rather than in dollars.\n\nThe reason households get it backwards is structural rather than stupid. The frequent risks are vivid, they are sold to you at the moment of purchase by a person who is standing there, and they come with a story about the last time something broke. The rare ones require imagining an event you have never experienced, there is nobody at the till, and the paperwork is unpleasant. So the observable pattern is four small policies covering things the household could pay for, and nothing at all on the earning capacity that pays for the four.",
  failureMode: "Four small policies plus every excess set as low as it will go, running to perhaps a hundred and forty dollars a month, which is seventeen hundred a year spent against risks whose worst outcome is an annoying fortnight. The same household has nothing covering the income that pays all four premiums, so the single event that would actually end the plan is the one risk left sitting on the household's own balance sheet.",
  worked: "Annual, two questions, run down a list you already have:\n\n\"Every July, list every premium I pay and every excess I have chosen. For each one answer: how often would this happen, and could I pay for the worst version out of the buffer without changing anything else. Cancel anything where the second answer is yes, and raise the excess anywhere the premium saving is real.\"\n\nThen the clause that stops this becoming a spending increase:\n\n\"Whatever the cancellations save goes straight onto the premium for the thing that would actually end the plan, or into the buffer, on the same day.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any comparison of providers, which is a different task, takes an afternoon, and is the thing people do instead of this one. Triage first and shop second, and if you only ever do one of them, do the triage.",
  experiment: "List every insurance premium and every excess you currently pay, read off the bank statement rather than recalled, and put the two questions beside each line. Most people find between one and three that fail, and the total is usually enough to pay for something that matters. Cancel one today and move the saving the same afternoon, because a saving with no destination is a spending increase with a virtuous story attached.",
  reflection: "Which policy failed the two questions, and where did the money go instead?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the money released by any policy I cancel today be paying for something specific in ninety days, rather than absorbed into everyday spending?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Insurance is priced above the expected loss, so buying it is a negative expected value trade. Why is that not an argument against buying it?",
    a: "Because the average is not what you are buying. An insurer must charge more than the expected loss to cover its costs and capital, so over many small claims the insured party pays a loading for a loss they could have carried. Where the loss would remove your capacity to continue, paying a loading to delete that branch is the right trade and expected value is simply the wrong measure of it.\n\nThat gives a test with no judgement in it: how often would this happen, and could you pay for the worst version out of the buffer without changing anything else. Yes to the second means absorb it. No means transfer it. Households get it backwards because the frequent risks are vivid and sold at the counter, while the rare ones require imagining something that has never happened to you."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every premium and excess I pay and here is what my buffer holds. Tell me which policies are a loading paid to avoid a cost I could carry, and what the released money should be buying instead."
},
{
  id: "shocks-human-capital-is-the-asset",
  track: "shocks", level: "annual",
  title: "Cover tracks what households are exposed to only weakly, and the largest thing you own is the income you have not earned yet",
  source: "Bernheim, Forni, Gokhale and Kotlikoff on the adequacy of life insurance holdings, using the United States Health and Retirement Study, read against the default income protection and total-and-permanent-disability cover held inside Australian superannuation",
  gateIntro: "A colleague was off work for five months last year with a back injury and mentioned, somewhere in the middle of it, that the insurance in her super had paid her something. You did not ask what, or after how long, or how she found out she had it. You hold the same kind of account she does and you have never once opened the insurance page inside it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the annual instruction that ends in four written facts about the cover inside your own fund, and name the fact you would check it against.",
  evidence: "A survey study rather than a trial. Bernheim, Forni, Gokhale and Kotlikoff took households in the Health and Retirement Study, computed for each how far a survivor's living standard would fall if a particular member died, and compared that exposure against the life cover actually held. Two things came out of it. A substantial share of the households facing the largest fall held little or no cover. And the relationship between exposure and cover was weak in both directions, with heavily insured households that had almost nothing to lose sitting alongside badly exposed households with nothing in place. One study, one cohort, one country, and the exposure calculation rests on a model of survivor needs that the authors had to choose. The Australian half of this entry is not from that paper at all: default income protection and total-and-permanent-disability cover inside superannuation is the most widely held insurance in the country that people do not know they hold, and the claim made about it here is only that its terms are rarely checked, which is an assertion about a population rather than a measurement of one.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "The present value of your remaining earnings is usually larger than everything else you own put together, and the cover on it is almost always a default that somebody else chose.",
  why: "Capitalise the wage and the balance sheet changes shape. For a mid-career earner the present value of the earnings still to come is generally larger than the house, the fund and the portfolio combined, and it is also the asset that produces the savings which buy the other three. It is the only major asset with no statement, no ticker, no quoted return and no anniversary, which is most of the reason it goes unexamined while three hours get spent on a fee comparison.\n\nWhat Bernheim and colleagues add is that the problem is not simply too little cover, it is that cover and exposure are only loosely related. That is worse news than a uniform shortfall would be. A shortfall means households understood the risk and under-bought, which a single adjustment fixes. A weak relationship in both directions means the household's own reading of its exposure is not the thing determining the cover. Something else is doing it: a form, an employer, a default, whoever last had a conversation with them about it.\n\nThe Australian shape of this is unusually tractable, because most working people already hold something and simply do not know its terms. Four facts decide whether what you hold is any use: how long you wait before it pays, how long it pays for, whether it tests you against your own occupation or any occupation at all, and what the premium is doing to the balance. Those take one login to establish. Reforms in recent years stopped default cover being switched on inside some new and low-balance accounts, which cuts both ways: fewer people paying premiums on forgotten accounts, and some people holding nothing while assuming otherwise.",
  failureMode: "You hold cover you have never read, with a waiting period of ninety days, against a buffer that covers six weeks of fixed costs. The gap the cover was supposed to close is open for six weeks and the buffer runs out inside it. Or the reverse: you hold nothing, because the account it sat in was consolidated three years ago and the cover did not travel with the balance, and you have been under the impression you were covered for the whole three years.",
  worked: "Annual, and it terminates in four written facts rather than a feeling:\n\n\"Every July, log in to my super fund and write down four things about any insurance inside it: the waiting period, the benefit period, whether it pays against my own occupation or any occupation, and the annual premium. Then put the waiting period beside the number of months my buffer actually covers.\"\n\nThat last clause is the whole instruction. The cover and the buffer are one system and they are almost always set independently of each other, so the join is where the hole is. A long waiting period demands a larger buffer, and a large buffer lets you accept a longer wait and a cheaper premium.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any decision about how much cover to hold, which is a conversation with a person and cannot be made annual. Establishing the four facts can be, and most people have never done it once.",
  experiment: "Log in to your superannuation account today and find the insurance section, which is usually one click from the balance. Write down the waiting period, the benefit period, the occupation definition and the annual premium. If one of the four is missing you may have no cover at all, which is the most useful thing you will learn this month. Then put the waiting period next to the months your buffer covers and see whether the two meet.",
  reflection: "What was the waiting period, and did your buffer reach it?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I be able to state my own waiting period and benefit period from memory in thirty days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Life cover tracked household exposure only weakly in this study. Why is a mismatch worse news than a uniform shortfall?",
    a: "Because a uniform shortfall would mean households read their exposure correctly and under-bought, which one adjustment fixes. A weak relationship in both directions, exposed households with nothing and unexposed households heavily covered, means the household's own assessment is not what determines the cover. A form, an employer, a default or whoever last sold them a policy is.\n\nThe Australian version is that most working people already hold income protection or total-and-permanent-disability cover inside superannuation on terms they did not choose and cannot state. Four facts decide whether it is any use: the waiting period, the benefit period, the occupation definition and the premium. The waiting period is the one that has to be checked against the buffer, because those two are one system and are almost always set apart from each other."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is what my super fund says about the insurance inside it and here is how many months my buffer covers. Tell me where the gap sits between the waiting period and the buffer, and what closing it costs from either end."
},
{
  id: "shocks-liquid-assets-waiting-period",
  track: "shocks", level: "annual",
  title: "The savings you built to bridge a job loss can push back the payment you were bridging to",
  source: "The liquid assets waiting period applying to JobSeeker Payment and related participation payments under the Social Security Act 1991, administered by Services Australia",
  gateIntro: "You are made redundant on a Wednesday, with a payment that has been calculated and has not landed. Somebody tells you to get onto the government payment straight away. You assume you will not qualify, because you have savings, and you assume that having savings is rather the point of having savings. It goes to the bottom of a list of eleven other things.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the two instructions this needs: the annual one that looks something up, and the day-one one that goes on your cut order.",
  evidence: "Statute rather than a finding. Australian social security law applies a liquid assets waiting period to JobSeeker Payment and some other participation payments: where a claimant's liquid assets exceed a legislated threshold at the time of claim, the start of payment is deferred, and the length of the deferral rises with the amount held up to a legislated maximum. Liquid assets are defined broadly enough to include cash at bank, term deposits and amounts owed to you, which brings a redundancy payment into the calculation before it has arrived. There is also a separate ordinary waiting period, and income and assets tests that may exclude a household entirely. This entry deliberately quotes no threshold and no maximum, because both are legislated numbers that move and neither is worth carrying in your head. What is stable is the mechanism: liquid savings postpone the payment. Check the current figures at the source, and note that hardship provisions exist which can shorten or waive the wait in defined circumstances.",
  replication: "statute",
  asAt: "2026-09-02",
  sourceUrl: "https://www.servicesaustralia.gov.au/liquid-assets-waiting-period",
  verifiedOn: "2026-09-02",
  cheat: "Before counting your buffer as runway, check the liquid assets waiting period: savings above a legislated threshold defer the start of JobSeeker.",
  idea: "The buffer and the payment it bridges to are not additive, because holding the buffer defers the payment, and the wait is served out of the savings rather than after them.",
  why: "Almost everybody models this the same way, and the model has an addition in it that does not hold. The savings cover you for some number of months, then the payment starts, so the runway is the sum. What actually happens is that the deferral is calculated from what you hold when you claim and is served while you are living on those holdings, so the payment does not begin where the savings end. The two resources overlap rather than stack, and the overlap is precisely the part you were counting on.\n\nThis is not an argument for holding less. The savings are still the thing keeping you solvent, and the payment is means tested on income and assets anyway, so for many mid-career households it is not arriving in the early months under any scenario. The error is arithmetic rather than strategic: two numbers being added that do not add, discovered in the week you are least equipped to discover anything.\n\nThe definition of liquid assets is the second surprise, because it reaches money owed to you as well as money you hold. So a redundancy payment counts before it has been paid, which means the same event that stops your income also creates the asset that defers the substitute. That interaction is genuinely counterintuitive, it is knowable in advance, and it is the reason this belongs in a track about pre-deciding the response. The general shape of what follows is to lodge the claim early rather than at the point of need, and to get the start date confirmed in writing rather than assumed.",
  failureMode: "You plan as though the buffer covers five months and the payment picks up afterwards, which reads on paper as something close to ten months of runway. In the event, the redundancy payment counted as a liquid asset before it arrived, the deferral was served out of the buffer rather than after it, and the payment began around the time the savings ran out rather than months later. The ten months was always five, and month six is where you find out.",
  worked: "Two instructions, and they belong in different places. The annual one:\n\n\"Every July, look up the current liquid assets waiting period and write down two figures: the level of liquid assets at which the deferral starts, and the longest it can run. Then answer one question: does my buffer cover its own months plus that maximum.\"\n\nAnd the response, which goes on the written cut order rather than in a diary:\n\n\"On the day I lose my income, lodge the claim immediately even if I expect to be refused, and get the payment start date in writing.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent from both is any figure copied into this entry. Thresholds move, and a number written down in 2026 and read back in 2029 is worse than no number, because it will be believed. The instruction is to look it up, not to remember it.",
  experiment: "Look up the current liquid assets waiting period today, at the source rather than through a forum, and find the two figures: where the deferral begins and how long it can run. Then set that against your buffer and answer whether it covers its own months plus the maximum wait. If it does not, you have just found the real size of the gap, and it is a different number from the one you have been using. Add the day-one line to your written cut order while you are in there.",
  reflection: "How long could the deferral run, and did your buffer cover its own months plus that?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is your buffer plus the government payment not the sum of the two?",
    a: "Because holding liquid savings defers the start of the payment. Where liquid assets exceed a legislated threshold at the time of claim, the start date is pushed back, and the deferral grows with the amount held up to a maximum, so the wait is served out of the buffer rather than beginning where the buffer ends. The two resources overlap instead of stacking.\n\nThe definition of liquid assets is also broader than people assume and reaches money owed to you, so a redundancy payment counts before it has arrived. The same event that stops your income creates the asset that defers the substitute. Both the threshold and the maximum are legislated figures that move, which is why the instruction is to look them up each year rather than to memorise them."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my buffer, my notice period and any redundancy entitlement. Work out my real runway if I lost my income tomorrow, taking the liquid assets waiting period into account, and tell me what I should be doing on day one."
},
{
  id: "shocks-health-shock-is-income-shock",
  track: "shocks", level: "annual",
  title: "In Australia the damage from a health shock arrives mostly as lost income rather than as a bill",
  source: "The institutional shape of Medicare and the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme, set against the United States medical bankruptcy literature that does not transfer to it",
  gateIntro: "The specialist appointment is on the twelfth and the referral says the scan will take about three weeks to come back. You are fine, probably. You have also worked out, lying awake on Sunday, that you have no idea how much sick leave you have accrued, that the payroll system shows it in hours, and that you have never converted that figure into anything meaningful.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the annual instruction that puts your sick leave, your waiting period and your buffer on the same page, in the units that matter.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is reasoning from institutional structure rather than from a study and should be read that way. Australia funds public hospital treatment and subsidises medical services and medicines through Medicare and the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme, with safety nets that cap out-of-pocket costs once thresholds are reached, so the catastrophic hospital bill that dominates the American literature is not the main channel here. What remains is real and less discussed: specialist fees charged above the schedule, dental and allied health outside the subsidy, treatments not on the list, travel for regional patients, and the distance between a public waiting list and the private alternative. The larger channel is income, both the patient's and a partner's reduced hours. The widely quoted American medical-bankruptcy figures came from surveys of people already in bankruptcy, and later work using hospital admission records recovered a far smaller causal share, so importing either the framing or the numbers would be an error. What this entry cannot offer is an Australian quasi-experimental estimate of the household income effect of a health shock, which is exactly why it is filed as craft.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Medicare changes the shape of the risk rather than removing it, and what it leaves exposed is your earnings, the gaps and your partner's hours.",
  why: "Be precise about what the institution does. It removes the tail risk of the hospital bill, which in a country without it is the single largest financial danger of illness. It does not pay you while you cannot work. It does not cover the whole of a specialist's fee. It does not cover the days a partner takes off to drive you to appointments. So the risk has been moved rather than deleted, from the medical side of the ledger to the income side, and the income side is the one nobody insures.\n\nThat matters for the buffer arithmetic in this track because an illness-driven income shock has worse parameters than a redundancy. There is no notice period. There is no payout. The duration is uncertain at the start and stays uncertain. New recurring costs arrive in the same month the income stops. And any cover you hold has a waiting period in front of it, which is the join back to the four facts inside your fund. The same buffer is being asked to bridge a longer and less predictable gap from a lower starting point.\n\nSo the annual review here is made of unglamorous items. How much paid sick leave has accrued, expressed in weeks rather than the hours payroll reports. The waiting period on any cover. Whether anybody else could pay the household's bills if you could not. And whether the person who would drop to three days a week to look after you has any income protection of their own, which is the item most consistently missed, because households insure the larger earner and then discover that one event took both incomes at once.",
  failureMode: "A diagnosis in February against three weeks of accrued sick leave and a ninety day waiting period on cover you did not know you held. That is nine weeks with nothing arriving, while a partner drops to three days to manage appointments, so household income falls by more than the salary that stopped. The treatment itself costs almost nothing that matters. By April the household is down eleven thousand dollars and none of it was a medical bill.",
  worked: "Annual, and it is four numbers on one page:\n\n\"Every July, write down my accrued sick leave in weeks, the waiting period on any income cover I hold, the same two figures for my partner, and the weeks of fixed costs the buffer covers. The distance between the first two is what the buffer has to bridge.\"\n\nThen one standing line, which is the part almost nobody writes down:\n\n\"Name the person who would have to pay the household's bills if I could not, and make sure they know where the one-page list is.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any attempt to estimate treatment costs. In this country that is the smaller and least predictable half of the problem, and estimating it is a good way to spend an afternoon not doing the arithmetic that matters.",
  experiment: "Find out today how much paid sick leave you have accrued and convert it into weeks, because payroll reports hours and weeks is the unit the shock arrives in. Put it beside the waiting period you wrote down from your fund. If there is a gap, that gap is the buffer's job, and it is a different gap from the redundancy one, because there is no notice period and no payout standing in front of it.",
  reflection: "How many weeks of sick leave do you actually hold, and how did it compare with the waiting period?",
  recall: {
    q: "Medicare covers hospital treatment, so why is a health shock still a financial shock here?",
    a: "Because what it removes is the bill, not the wage. Public hospital treatment and subsidised services and medicines take away the catastrophic-bill tail that dominates the American literature, and they do nothing about the income that stops, the specialist fees charged above the schedule, the dental and allied health outside the subsidy, or the hours a partner drops to manage it. The risk moves from the medical side of the ledger to the income side, which is the side nobody insures.\n\nAnd an illness-driven income shock has worse parameters than a redundancy: no notice period, no payout, an uncertain duration, new recurring costs in the same month, and a waiting period sitting in front of any cover. The same buffer bridges a longer gap from a lower starting point."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my sick leave, my income cover and my partner's. Work out how long our household could actually absorb an illness that stopped one of us working, and tell me which of the four numbers to fix first."
},
{
  id: "shocks-relationship-breakdown",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "Separation is associated with the largest single-event fall in household wealth, and the arrow points both ways",
  source: "Zagorsky, Marriage and divorce's impact on wealth (Journal of Sociology, 2005), using the United States National Longitudinal Survey of Youth",
  gateIntro: "A friend's marriage ended in March and she is at your kitchen table explaining that she does not know which fund holds her superannuation, has never seen the loan statement, and has been asked by a solicitor for a list of assets she cannot produce. Her husband was not hiding anything from her. He simply did all of it, for nineteen years, and she let him.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the recurring instruction that would have meant she could produce that list, including how often it runs and who holds a copy.",
  evidence: "United States panel data rather than an experiment. Zagorsky followed a cohort in the National Longitudinal Survey of Youth and compared wealth across marital statuses over time. Married respondents held several times the wealth of never-married respondents of the same age, and divorced respondents held dramatically less, a gap usually reported at around three-quarters. The detail that matters most is the timing: wealth among those who went on to divorce was already falling for years beforehand. That is what makes the direction of causation genuinely rather than rhetorically unclear. Splitting a household destroys wealth mechanically, through legal costs, duplicated housing and assets sold on a timetable nobody chose. Financial strain is also one of the better-documented correlates of relationship breakdown, so part of the earlier fall may be contributing to the event. The design cannot separate the two, selection into marriage differs from selection into divorce on many unobserved dimensions, and this is one American cohort born inside a narrow window.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "The money and the relationship are not independent systems, and the arrangement that helps under both outcomes is knowing where everything is.",
  why: "The mechanical destruction is real and can be listed without anybody behaving badly. One household becomes two, carrying two sets of housing costs against the same total income. Assets are sold on a schedule set by a settlement rather than by anybody's judgement about timing. Legal costs come out of capital rather than out of income. And the long-horizon assets get divided at an age where there is materially less time left to rebuild them, which is the part that compounds.\n\nThe pre-existing decline is what changes the response. If wealth is already falling for years before the event, then some of the association is strain contributing to the breakdown rather than the breakdown producing the loss, and nothing in a panel like this can apportion the two. What it does establish is that these are one system, which means that arrangements reducing financial conflict inside a household are doing two jobs at once and should not be evaluated as though they only did one.\n\nWhich produces an unusual conclusion for a track full of instructions. The useful install is not a hedge against separation, which is both offensive and largely unavailable. It is transparency during the relationship: both people knowing what comes in, what goes out, what is owed and where everything sits. That has value if the relationship continues, because opacity is itself a reliable source of argument and a single point of failure. It has value if it ends, because the person who does not know where the assets are is the person disadvantaged in the settlement and the one who spends three months at hourly rates finding out what they already owned. There is no version of the arrangement that is worse for having done it.",
  failureMode: "One person in the household has never seen the loan statement, cannot name the fund holding their own superannuation, does not know the offset balance and has no login to anything. If the relationship continues, that is a standing source of conflict and a complete single point of failure the day the other person is hit by a bus. If it ends, it is three months of discovery paid at hourly rates, starting from not knowing what is being divided.",
  worked: "A shared document rather than a transfer, and the frequency is what makes it survive:\n\n\"Twice a year we sit down for thirty minutes with one page: every account, every loan, every fund, every policy, the balance of each and who can access it. Both of us keep a copy and both of us have the logins.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any budgeting, any review of what either person spent, and any decision. Thirty minutes on the inventory, nothing else. The version of this meeting that includes a conversation about the weekend's spending is the version that gets cancelled in month two and never rescheduled, which is how a household ends up nineteen years into an arrangement only one person understands.\n\nIf you are the one who currently holds all of it, notice that this instruction is least comfortable and most necessary for you.",
  experiment: "Write the one-page inventory today, alone if you have to: every account, loan, fund, insurance policy and liability, with balances and who can access each. Then give a copy to the person it would matter to. Most households where one person does the money have never produced this page, it takes about twenty minutes, and it reliably surfaces at least one thing the other person did not know existed.",
  reflection: "Could the other person in your household have produced that page without you?",
  recall: {
    q: "Wealth was already falling for years before the divorces in this data, not only afterwards. Why does that change what the finding is good for?",
    a: "Because it stops the association being readable as the event causing the loss. Part of the damage is certainly mechanical: one household becomes two against the same income, assets are sold on a settlement's timetable, legal costs come out of capital, and long-horizon assets are divided at an age with less time to rebuild. But financial strain is also a well-documented correlate of breakdown, so part of the earlier decline may be contributing to the event, and one American cohort in a panel cannot apportion the two.\n\nWhat survives is that the money and the relationship are one system, which points at an install that is good under either outcome: both people knowing where every account, loan and fund sits. Opacity is a source of conflict if the relationship continues and a disadvantage in the settlement if it does not."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me build the one-page inventory of every account, loan, fund and policy in our household, and tell me what my partner would not be able to find without me."
},
{
  id: "shocks-uninsurable-absorbed-structurally",
  track: "shocks", level: "judgement",
  title: "The largest risks you face cannot be insured at all, and the only remaining response is structural",
  source: "The insurability conditions used in underwriting, and the practice of absorbing residual risk through structure rather than transferring it",
  gateIntro: "Two people you trained with have left the industry inside eighteen months, and the second one said at her leaving drinks that there was no work in it any more, not the way there had been. You have a mortgage sized on your current salary, fees committed to the end of next year, and a skill that perhaps three employers in this city buy.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the annual instruction that measures what you cannot exit quickly, and name the first commitment you would remove.",
  evidence: "No trial. Insurability is a set of conditions rather than a finding: a risk can be transferred when the loss is definite and measurable, when events are largely independent across policyholders, when the probability can be estimated, and when neither adverse selection nor moral hazard makes the pool unpriceable. Several of the largest risks a household faces fail at least one of those conditions outright. An industry or a region contracting hits everyone who would have bought the cover at the same time, so there is no pool. A relationship ending is neither definable in advance nor independent of the insured's own conduct. A dependent needing support for decades is a certainty rather than a risk for the households it affects, so the premium approaches the loss. Sustained inflation arrives for all policyholders at once. None of that is contestable the way a finding is, and none of it says how much structure you should hold, which is the actual question and the reason this is a judgement rather than an instruction.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "A risk that is correlated, undefinable or certain cannot be handed to anybody, so what absorbs it is liquidity, redundancy, flexibility and the height of your fixed commitments.",
  why: "The failure of insurability is a category rather than a gap in the market. Insurance works by pooling draws that are close to independent, so when the event arrives for everybody in the pool at once there is nobody left to pay for it. When it cannot be defined in advance there is no contract to write. When it is certain, the premium is simply the loss with an administration fee on top. Which means the class of risk that cannot be transferred is not an obscure corner; it contains most of what people actually lie awake about, including the industry, the city, the marriage, the dependent, the long inflation and the change that removes what you are paid for.\n\nWhat absorbs those instead is structure, and it comes in four parts, none of which looks like a product. Liquidity, which buys time to respond rather than covering the loss. Redundancy in income, whether that is a second earner, a second skill or a second source. Flexibility, meaning real optionality about where you live and what work you will take. And low fixed commitments, which lower the level you have to keep clearing every month. Each of those is paid for in current consumption or current earnings, which is exactly why they lose every argument to an insurance product: the product has a premium you can quote and these have a cost you feel.\n\nThe judgement is how much of your present life to spend on structure against risks nobody can price, and there is no evidence that answers it. One thing can be said directionally. The households that come through uninsurable events intact are consistently the ones whose fixed commitments were low rather than the ones who had better products, and the fixed commitments are the only item on the list that you set deliberately, in writing, on a date you can identify. If you act on one part of this, act on the height of the water.",
  failureMode: "Your industry contracts over about three years in a way nobody in it insured against, because nobody could. What determines the outcome is entirely structural: a mortgage sized on the peak salary, fees committed for eight more years, a city you cannot leave for family reasons, and a skill only that industry buys. Every one of those was a defensible decision on the day it was made, and together they set your fixed costs at a level that requires the industry to keep existing.",
  worked: "No instruction carries this one, which is the honest answer, so what follows is the closest available thing:\n\n\"Once a year, write the annual total of every commitment I could not exit inside three months, and how many months my liquidity covers that total alone. Then answer in writing: which single commitment would I remove first, and what would removing it require.\"\n\nThat second sentence is the exercise. Not reducing the commitments, which is a decision with other people inside it, but knowing which one goes first and what it would take, worked out on a quiet afternoon rather than in the month it matters.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any target for the ratio, because nobody has one and anybody offering you one is selling something. What the instruction produces is a number you have actually looked at and a first move you have already chosen.",
  experiment: "Add up every commitment you could not exit inside three months: the mortgage or rent, fees, contracts, anything with a break cost. Write the annual figure, divide by twelve, and write how many months your liquidity would cover that figure alone. Then write the name of the commitment you would remove first and one sentence on what removing it needs. Most people have never named it, and the naming is most of the value here.",
  reflection: "Which commitment did you name as the first to go, and what would removing it actually take?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why can some of the largest household risks not be insured, and what takes the place of insurance when they cannot?",
    a: "Because insurance pools draws that are close to independent, and these risks fail the conditions pooling needs. An industry or a region contracting arrives for everyone who would have bought the cover at once, so there is nobody left to pay. A relationship ending is neither definable in advance nor independent of the insured's own conduct. A dependent needing lifelong support is a certainty rather than a risk for the households it affects, so the premium is the loss.\n\nWhat replaces transfer is structure: liquidity, which buys time rather than covering the loss; redundancy in income; flexibility about where you live and what you will do; and low fixed commitments, which lower the level you have to keep clearing. All four are paid for in current consumption, and none can be quoted as a premium, which is why they lose every argument to a product. The one you set deliberately and can revisit is the commitments."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my fixed commitments and my liquidity. Tell me which of my biggest risks cannot be insured at all, and rank my commitments by how hard each one would be to exit."
}
);
